      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "type": "llm_response",
      "reasoning": "Hmm, let me look at this hand carefully. I've got:\n\
- 2 Forests (lands)\n\
- 2 Talisman of Impulse (2-mana artifacts for ramp)\n\
- Malevolent Rumble (2-mana spell)\n\
- Icetill Explorer (4-mana 2/4 creature)\n\
- Emrakul, the Promised End (13-mana bomb)\n\
\n\
This is a 7-card hand with only 2 lands. While I have ramp pieces (the Talismans), I need 2 mana to cast them first, which means I need to hit my land drops. With only 2 lands in a 7-card hand, this is risky - I could easily miss land drops and get stuck.\n\
\n\
However, if I mulligan to 6, I'm not guaranteed a better hand. Let me think about the curve:\n\
- Turn 1: Forest, pass\n\
- Turn 2: Forest, Talisman (if I draw a land, I can cast the other Talisman or Rumble)\n\
- Turn 3+: Need more lands to function\n\
\n\
This hand has some play to it if I draw lands, but it's land-light. In the old days, we'd keep marginal hands and like it! But competitively, 2 lands with no card draw in the opener is concerning.\n\
\n\
Actually, wait - I should keep this. I have two 2-mana ramp pieces, so if I draw even one more land in my first few draws, I can start deploying my Talismans and potentially curve out. The hand has a plan and payoffs.",
