  "game_over": {
    "seq": 468,
    "message": "Player Sonnet Gramps is the winner",
  },
  "youtube_url": "https://youtu.be/gIGKB4oJ9-o",
  "annotations": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Fetching Raucous Theater (enters tapped) instead of Blood Crypt (can enter untapped for 2 life) means they cannot cast any spells on turn 1 despite having Faithless Looting and Thoughtseize in hand. Turn 1 Thoughtseize is extremely valuable for disruption, and Faithless Looting would advance their graveyard plan.",
      "action_taken": "Fetched Raucous Theater, which enters tapped, preventing any turn 1 spell casting",
      "better_line": "Fetch Blood Crypt, pay 2 life to have it enter untapped, play Island from hand, then cast Thoughtseize (or Faithless Looting) on turn 1 for early disruption/setup",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 9,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 14,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarding Island when only having 1 land on the battlefield and desperately needing to make land drops. With two copies of the legendary Gran-Gran in hand (only one can be on the battlefield at a time), discarding a duplicate legendary is better than discarding a land when severely mana-starved.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Island, keeping both copies of legendary Gran-Gran",
      "better_line": "Discard one Gran-Gran (legendary duplicate) to preserve land drops, as having only 1 land in play makes every land critical for development.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 21,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 23,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "With Sire of Seven Deaths (7 mana, colorless Eldrazi) and Emrakul in hand, Ugin's Labyrinth would have been significantly better. Ugin's Labyrinth can imprint a colorless card with mana value 7+ (like Sire of Seven Deaths or Emrakul) to produce 2 colorless mana per tap, effectively acting as a Sol land. The player already has multiple colorless Eldrazi to imprint. Eldrazi Temple only produces 2 colorless mana specifically for Eldrazi spells/abilities, while Ugin's Labyrinth does the same with imprint but is a land that can be played immediately and doesn't have the Eldrazi-only restriction on the bonus mana. More importantly, the player put TWO copies of Ugin's Labyrinth into the graveyard by not picking one, losing access to both. With Icetill Explorer in hand (which lets you play lands from graveyard), one copy could potentially be recovered, but having it in hand is still better. Ugin's Labyrinth with imprint would have provided faster acceleration toward casting Emrakul or Sire.",
      "action_taken": "Selected Eldrazi Temple over Ugin's Labyrinth from Malevolent Rumble's revealed cards.",
      "better_line": "Select Ugin's Labyrinth, which when played can imprint Sire of Seven Deaths or Emrakul to produce 2 colorless mana per tap — equally good or better acceleration for the Eldrazi game plan, while preserving the second Ugin's Labyrinth in graveyard for potential recovery with Icetill Explorer.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 23,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 28,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Took Sire of Seven Deaths instead of Emrakul, the Promised End with Thoughtseize. Emrakul's cast trigger (controlling opponent's next turn) is essentially game-ending, and taking Sire puts a creature card type in the graveyard, reducing Emrakul's cost from 9 to 8. The opponent has Icetill Explorer enabling land replay from graveyard (including 2x Ugin's Labyrinth which can imprint Emrakul for massive mana), making casting Emrakul very realistic.",
      "action_taken": "Chose to discard Sire of Seven Deaths",
      "better_line": "Discard Emrakul, the Promised End - it's the most powerful and game-warping threat, its cast trigger is nearly unbeatable, and leaving it in hand while reducing its cost by discarding Sire is counterproductive.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 39,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 41,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "GLM47 Coach passed the turn with 3 mana available (Steam Vents + Polluted Delta for a land) and multiple playable cards. Gran-Gran costs {U} and could have been cast off Steam Vents, providing a body that loots when tapped and potentially enables cost reduction later. The opponent has an Icetill Explorer (2/4) that is generating value through land-from-graveyard and mill, and a Sire of Seven Deaths in the graveyard that could be reanimated or recast. Developing the board with Gran-Gran would help find answers and apply pressure. Additionally, Faithless Looting could have been cast to improve hand quality and stock the graveyard for Persist (which could target the opponent's Sire of Seven Deaths from their graveyard - though Persist only works on your own graveyard). At minimum, casting Gran-Gran for {U} to develop the board was free and had no downside.",
      "action_taken": "Passed the turn without casting any spells, leaving mana unused.",
      "better_line": "Cast Gran-Gran for {U} off Steam Vents to develop the board and enable future looting triggers. Could also consider casting Faithless Looting to improve hand quality and fill graveyard.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 42,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 45,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Emperor of Bones before combat is suboptimal sequencing. Emperor of Bones has an 'at the beginning of combat on your turn' trigger that exiles a card from a graveyard, which won't trigger this turn since the precombat main phase hasn't moved to combat yet — wait, actually the combat trigger will still happen this turn since it triggers at the beginning of combat. However, casting a creature before combat when holding Fatal Push and Bitter Triumph (trick-style cards) gives the opponent information unnecessarily. More importantly, the player should consider sequencing: they have Polluted Delta uncracked, and should likely crack it first to enable Revolt for Fatal Push (which could kill Icetill Explorer, a 2/4 with mana value 4). Playing Emperor of Bones before cracking Polluted Delta means if they need Fatal Push with Revolt later this turn, they've used mana on the creature first. Additionally, Faithless Looting could be cast first to improve hand quality and potentially set up better plays, since Emperor of Bones wants good targets in graveyards to reanimate.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Emperor of Bones as the first action in precombat main phase",
      "better_line": "Crack Polluted Delta first to enable Revolt for Fatal Push, then consider casting Faithless Looting to filter the hand (discarding cards like Persist or extra Gran-Grans), before deciding whether to deploy Emperor of Bones or hold up removal mana.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 46,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 53,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Should play a land from graveyard first (via Icetill Explorer) before casting Sowing Mycospawn. Playing Eldrazi Temple from graveyard would add extra mana and trigger landfall/mill, potentially enabling additional plays this turn (like also casting Talisman of Impulse). Land drops should come before spell casting for maximum mana efficiency.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Sowing Mycospawn before playing a land from graveyard",
      "better_line": "Play Eldrazi Temple (or Ugin's Labyrinth) from graveyard first via Icetill Explorer's ability, trigger landfall mill, then cast Sowing Mycospawn with more mana available for the same turn",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 54,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 62,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Bitter Triumph in response to their own Faithless Looting means they must discard a card (or pay 3 life) before seeing the 2 cards Faithless Looting would draw. By letting Looting resolve first, they'd have 2 additional cards to choose from as discard fodder for both Bitter Triumph and Looting's discard, maximizing card selection.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Bitter Triumph before Faithless Looting resolved, requiring an immediate discard from the current hand.",
      "better_line": "Let Faithless Looting resolve first (draw 2, discard 2), then cast Bitter Triumph on Icetill Explorer, discarding the worst remaining card. This maximizes card quality across both discard effects.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 58,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 65,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded Psychic Frog (a premier win condition and card advantage engine) instead of one of the two duplicate Gran-Grans in hand. Gran-Gran without Lessons in graveyard is just a 1/2 looter, and having two copies makes one very expendable. Psychic Frog is significantly more impactful as a threat that can grow, gain flying, and draw cards.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Psychic Frog as the additional cost for Bitter Triumph",
      "better_line": "Discard one of the two Gran-Gran copies instead, preserving the more powerful Psychic Frog as a win condition",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 59,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 65,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Discarding Fatal Push over a second copy of Gran-Gran is debatable. Fatal Push is premium removal that could deal with future threats, while having two Gran-Gran in hand is redundant (legendary creature). Against an Eldrazi ramp deck, Fatal Push may not line up perfectly with their biggest threats, but it still has value against Sowing Mycospawn (MV 4 with revolt) or other mid-sized creatures.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Swamp and Fatal Push, keeping Persist, two Gran-Gran, and Undercity Sewers.",
      "better_line": "Discard one Gran-Gran (legendary, redundant copy) and Swamp, keeping Fatal Push for interaction alongside Persist, Gran-Gran, and Undercity Sewers.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 62,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 69,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Exiled Psychic Frog (1/2) with Emperor of Bones instead of Devourer of Destiny (6/6) from opponent's graveyard. When Emperor of Bones adapts and reanimates the exiled creature, a 6/6 hasty attacker is far more impactful than a 1/2 Psychic Frog (which would need hand cards discarded to pump and gets sacrificed at end step regardless). This also has the secondary benefit of denying the opponent a powerful card from their graveyard.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Psychic Frog from own graveyard with Emperor of Bones' combat trigger",
      "better_line": "Exile Devourer of Destiny from opponent's graveyard - it's a 6/6 that would deal massive damage when reanimated with haste via Adapt, and denies the opponent access to it",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 63,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 69,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Sonnet Gramps had enough mana to cast Sowing Mycospawn with kicker but chose to cast it unkicked. With Forest+Utopia Sprawl (GG), Forest (G), Stomping Ground (G/R), Eldrazi Temple (CC for colorless Eldrazi), and Eldrazi Spawn (C), they had at least 7 mana available, more than enough for the kicked cost of {3}{G}{1}{C} (6 total). Kicking it would exile one of the opponent's three lands, which would be devastating against an opponent at only 3 lands with Emperor of Bones as their only threat.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Sowing Mycospawn without kicker, only getting the land search effect.",
      "better_line": "Cast Sowing Mycospawn with kicker using Eldrazi Temple's CC (Mycospawn is devoid/colorless Eldrazi) plus other mana sources, searching for a land AND exiling one of the opponent's three lands to cripple their mana development.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 64,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 71,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Bojuka Bog enters tapped, preventing Emrakul from being cast this turn. With 5 card types in graveyard (land, instant/kindred, sorcery, artifact, creature), Emrakul costs 8. Current mana production is 7 (Forest+Sprawl=2, Forest=1, Stomping Ground=1, Eldrazi Temple=2 for Eldrazi, Spawn=1). Playing Verdant Catacombs (fetching untapped) or Sanctum of Ugin would provide the 8th mana needed to cast Emrakul this turn, gaining control of the opponent's next turn.",
      "action_taken": "Played Bojuka Bog, which enters tapped, to exile opponent's graveyard",
      "better_line": "Play Verdant Catacombs (or Sanctum of Ugin) to have 8 mana available and cast Emrakul, the Promised End this turn. Sanctum of Ugin would be best as it also triggers to tutor another colorless creature when Emrakul is cast. Exiling opponent's graveyard with Bojuka Bog provides marginal value compared to casting a 13/13 and taking control of opponent's turn.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 70,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 74,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Chose to reanimate Psychic Frog (1/2) instead of Sire of Seven Deaths (7/7 with first strike, menace, trample, lifelink, haste). Sire would have been nearly unblockable with menace (opponent only has Sowing Mycospawn and an Eldrazi Spawn token), would deal significant trample damage, and gain 7 life with lifelink. The creature gets sacrificed at end of turn regardless, so maximum immediate impact is what matters.",
      "action_taken": "Put Psychic Frog (1/2) onto the battlefield with Emperor of Bones trigger",
      "better_line": "Put Sire of Seven Deaths onto the battlefield - it attacks as a 7/7 haste menace trample lifelink creature, likely dealing 7 damage (or at minimum 3+ trample damage through blocks) while gaining 7 life, a massive swing compared to a 1/2 Psychic Frog",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 72,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 78,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded a land (Undercity Sewers) when at only 3 lands on board, rather than discarding one of two copies of the legendary Gran-Gran. With only 3 lands, the 4th land drop is important for deploying threats and casting spells. Since Gran-Gran is legendary, the second copy has significantly reduced value.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Undercity Sewers to Psychic Frog's pump ability",
      "better_line": "Discard one of the two Gran-Gran copies instead, preserving the land drop to develop mana while discarding a card with diminished value due to the legendary rule",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 74,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 81,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Gran-Gran has 'Whenever Gran-Gran becomes tapped, draw a card, then discard a card.' Attacking with Gran-Gran provides a free loot trigger. The opponent's only meaningful blocker is Sowing Mycospawn (3/3), which they're unlikely to use on a 1/2. The Eldrazi Spawn (0/1) could chump but that's a fine trade. The player also has a duplicate Gran-Gran in hand, reducing the risk of losing it.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only Emperor of Bones, leaving Gran-Gran back",
      "better_line": "Attack with both Emperor of Bones and Gran-Gran to get a free loot trigger from Gran-Gran's tap ability, filtering through the hand which contains a duplicate Gran-Gran",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 81,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 87,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Sonnet Gramps chose not to block Emperor of Bones (4/4) with the Eldrazi Spawn Token (0/1), which would have absorbed no damage but could have chumped to save 4 life. At 17 life going to 13, this matters because the opponent has an aggressive deck. The Eldrazi Spawn token provides {C} when sacrificed, but with Emrakul in hand and a developing board, saving 4 life by chump blocking with a 0/1 token that provides only 1 colorless mana seems like the better trade. The mana from the spawn is relatively minor compared to 4 life points.",
      "action_taken": "Declined to block Emperor of Bones, taking 4 damage (17 -> 13 life)",
      "better_line": "Chump block with Eldrazi Spawn Token to preserve 4 life points. The token's sacrifice ability for {C} is worth less than 4 life in most scenarios, especially against an aggressive opponent.",
      "type": "blunder",
