    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Eldrazi Temple instead of Sanctum of Ugin when both provide enough mana to cast the discounted Emrakul this turn. Sanctum of Ugin would trigger on casting Emrakul (colorless spell with MV 7+), allowing a sacrifice to tutor for another colorless creature, providing significant card advantage.",
      "action_taken": "Played Eldrazi Temple as their land for the turn.",
      "better_line": "Play Sanctum of Ugin instead. With Forest, Forest+Utopia Sprawl, Eldrazi Temple (2 colorless for Eldrazi), Stomping Ground, Bojuka Bog, Sanctum (1 colorless), and Eldrazi Spawn token (1 colorless), there's still enough mana to cast the 7-cost Emrakul, and Sanctum's trigger would let you tutor for another colorless creature.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 101,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 112,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Emrakul, the Promised End costs 7 mana (13 minus 6 card types in graveyard: land, instant, sorcery, artifact, creature, kindred). With Forest+Utopia Sprawl (2), Forest (1), Eldrazi Temple (2 for Eldrazi), Stomping Ground (1), Bojuka Bog (1), and Eldrazi Spawn sacrifice (1) = 8 mana available, Emrakul could be cast this turn while the opponent is completely tapped out. Emrakul's mind control trigger plus a 13/13 flying trampler would likely win the game.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Icetill Explorer, a 2/4 creature for 4 mana",
      "better_line": "Cast Emrakul, the Promised End for 7 mana, gaining control of the opponent's next turn and deploying a 13/13 flying trampler while the opponent is tapped out with no way to interact",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 102,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 113,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Exiled Persist (a sorcery) with Emperor of Bones instead of Abhorrent Oculus (a 5/5 flying creature). Emperor of Bones can only reanimate creature cards exiled with it, so exiling Persist accomplishes nothing. With Abhorrent Oculus exiled, they could adapt Emperor of Bones for {1}{B} (mana available from untapped lands), putting a 4/4 flying Oculus onto the battlefield with haste. Combined with potentially pumping Psychic Frog by discarding the Psychic Frog from hand, they could attack for significant flying damage against an opponent at 9 life. Instead they skipped attacks entirely.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Persist (a sorcery) with Emperor of Bones, then skipped attacking.",
      "better_line": "Exile Abhorrent Oculus with Emperor of Bones, then pay {1}{B} to adapt Emperor (getting +1/+1 counters), which puts Abhorrent Oculus onto the battlefield as a 4/4 flyer with haste. Discard Psychic Frog from hand to pump battlefield Psychic Frog to 2/3 and give it flying. Attack with both flyers for 6 damage, putting opponent to 3 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 106,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 115,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "At 9 life facing Emperor of Bones, Gran-Gran, and Psychic Frog, casting Talisman of Impulse for ramp is lower priority than addressing the board. Additionally, land should be played before casting spells - Sanctum of Ugin is particularly valuable as it synergizes with casting Emrakul later. Kozilek's Return would clear all three opposing creatures (all have 2 or less toughness) and set up the graveyard trigger for later Emrakul cast.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Talisman of Impulse for incremental ramp",
      "better_line": "Play Sanctum of Ugin (or Verdant Catacombs) as land drop first, then consider casting Kozilek's Return to clear Emperor of Bones, Gran-Gran, and Psychic Frog, protecting the 9 life total while also putting Kozilek's Return in graveyard for its Emrakul-triggered 5-damage ability later.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 107,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 117,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Emperor of Bones can only reanimate creature cards exiled with it when it gets +1/+1 counters via Adapt. Exiling Malevolent Rumble (a sorcery) means Emperor of Bones cannot reanimate anything. Abhorrent Oculus (5/5 flyer) was available in their own graveyard and could have been exiled, then reanimated with haste by paying {1}{B} for Adapt 2. With opponent at 9 life, a 5/5 flying haste attacker alongside existing creatures could threaten lethal.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Malevolent Rumble (a sorcery) from opponent's graveyard with Emperor of Bones trigger",
      "better_line": "Exile Abhorrent Oculus from own graveyard, then use Adapt 2 ({1}{B}) on Emperor of Bones to reanimate it as a 5/5 flying haste creature for a massive attack with opponent at 9 life",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 110,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 120,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded the second Psychic Frog to pump the battlefield Frog, when Faithless Looting was also in hand. Faithless Looting has flashback ({2}{R}), so discarding it loses almost nothing since it can still be cast from the graveyard. The second Psychic Frog is a real backup threat/additional attacker that is permanently lost by discarding it.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Psychic Frog from hand to put a +1/+1 counter on the battlefield Psychic Frog",
      "better_line": "Discard Faithless Looting instead to pump Psychic Frog - Faithless Looting retains its flashback value from the graveyard, while preserving the second Psychic Frog as a future threat",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 110,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 120,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Attacking Emperor of Bones into Sowing Mycospawn (3/3) risks losing a valuable creature that provides graveyard interaction and reanimation. Opponent is at 9 life, so 4 total damage isn't close to lethal, and losing Emperor of Bones to a bad trade weakens the board significantly.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with all three creatures including Emperor of Bones into a board with Sowing Mycospawn 3/3",
      "better_line": "Attack with only Gran-Gran (for the draw/discard trigger) and Psychic Frog (for potential card draw on damage), keeping Emperor of Bones back as a blocker against the 3/3 Mycospawn and preserving its graveyard exile and reanimation abilities.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 113,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 140,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Only blocking Emperor of Bones with Icetill Explorer leaves Sonnet Gramps at 2 life (taking 7 from unblocked Psychic Frog + Gran-Gran). At 2 life they're extremely vulnerable to any incidental damage. Sowing Mycospawn could have also blocked Psychic Frog to preserve 6 life.",
      "action_taken": "Blocked only Emperor of Bones with Icetill Explorer, going to 2 life",
      "better_line": "Block Emperor of Bones with Icetill Explorer AND Psychic Frog with Sowing Mycospawn. This preserves 6 life (going to 8 instead of 2), which is far more important than preserving a 3/3 body when at such a low life total facing aggressive creatures.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 114,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 133,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Emperor of Bones is the more dangerous threat on the opponent's board - it can exile creatures from Sonnet Gramps' graveyard (including Icetill Explorer) and reanimate them with haste, plus it can adapt to grow. Gran-Gran is just a 1/2 that loots when tapped. Blocking the lesser threat leaves the more impactful creature alive.",
      "action_taken": "Blocked Gran-Gran (1/2 value engine) instead of Emperor of Bones (2/2 recursive reanimator)",
      "better_line": "Block Emperor of Bones to kill the more dangerous recursive threat that can exploit graveyard creatures",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 114,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 133,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Kozilek's Return would deal 2 damage to all creatures, killing Emperor of Bones (2/2), Gran-Gran (1/2), and possibly Psychic Frog, while keeping Sowing Mycospawn (3/3) alive. Additionally, putting Kozilek's Return in the graveyard sets up its powerful second ability with Emrakul, the Promised End in hand - when Emrakul is cast, Kozilek's Return can be exiled from graveyard to deal 5 damage to all creatures.",
      "action_taken": "Blocked with Sowing Mycospawn, trading the 3/3 body for one attacker while still taking damage from the others",
      "better_line": "Cast Kozilek's Return to deal 2 damage to all creatures, clearing multiple attackers while preserving Sowing Mycospawn, and putting Kozilek's Return in the graveyard for its Emrakul synergy",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 114,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 133,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Sonnet Gramps is at 3 life with only Sowing Mycospawn (3/3) to block. GLM47 Coach has Psychic Frog (1/2), Emperor of Bones (2/2), and Gran-Gran (1/2). By discarding one card to pump Psychic Frog to 2/3, then attacking with all three (2+2+1=5 power), Sonnet Gramps can only block one creature. Blocking either 2-power creature lets 2+1=3 through for lethal. Even without pump, if opponent blocks the 2/2, only 2 gets through, but with the pump making two 2-power threats, any single block lets exactly 3 through.",
      "action_taken": "Skipped the attack phase without attacking, missing lethal on board.",
      "better_line": "Discard a card to Psychic Frog to give it a +1/+1 counter (making it 2/3), then attack with all three creatures (Psychic Frog, Emperor of Bones, Gran-Gran) for lethal damage since opponent can only block one.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 155,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "While controlling the opponent's turn via Emrakul, Sonnet Gramps passed without using Psychic Frog's activated ability to exile 3 cards from the opponent's graveyard (which included Abhorrent Oculus, Gran-Gran, Emperor of Bones). Exiling these cards would strip the opponent of powerful reanimation targets and future graveyard synergies at no real cost.",
      "action_taken": "Passed without activating any of the opponent's abilities",
      "better_line": "Activate Psychic Frog's 'Exile three cards from your graveyard: gains flying' ability to remove key cards (especially Abhorrent Oculus) from the opponent's graveyard, permanently denying reanimation/recursion targets",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 128,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 158,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "During the Emrakul control turn's end step, the player passed without activating any of GLM47 Coach's abilities. Gran-Gran could be tapped to draw/discard (letting the controller choose what to discard), and Psychic Frog could discard cards from their hand to waste resources. These activations would strip the opponent's hand of valuable cards before they get their extra turn.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without activating any of the opponent's abilities during the Emrakul-controlled end step.",
      "better_line": "Tap Gran-Gran to force a draw/discard (discarding their best card), then use Psychic Frog to discard remaining cards from the opponent's hand, stripping their resources before they take their extra turn.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 129,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 158,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Opponent is at 3 life. Abhorrent Oculus (mana value 3) is a legal Unearth target and would enter as a 5/5 flyer - since Unearth puts it onto the battlefield without casting, the 'exile six cards' additional cost doesn't apply. A 5/5 flyer is lethal on its own against an opponent at 3 life with no flying blockers. Instead, Gran-Gran (1/2) was chosen, which provides minimal board impact.",
      "action_taken": "Chose Gran-Gran (1/2) as Unearth target",
      "better_line": "Choose Abhorrent Oculus as Unearth target, getting a 5/5 flyer for free, then attack with the flyer for lethal damage against the opponent at 3 life",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 131,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 161,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Opponent is at 3 life with only Sowing Mycospawn (3/3) as a blocker. By discarding an Archon of Cruelty to Psychic Frog to make it 2/3, then attacking with all three creatures (Emperor 2/2, Frog 2/3, Gran-Gran 1/2), no matter which creature the opponent blocks with Mycospawn, the remaining two deal at least 3 damage, which is lethal.",
      "action_taken": "Declined to activate Psychic Frog's discard ability during combat",
      "better_line": "Discard Archon of Cruelty to Psychic Frog to pump it to 2/3, then attack with all three creatures for guaranteed lethal regardless of blocks",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 134,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 164,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Opponent is at 3 life with no creatures on the battlefield. Emperor of Bones (2/2) can Adapt 2 for {1}{B} to become 4/4, and Gran-Gran is a 1/2. Attacking with both deals 5 damage to an empty board, which is lethal against an opponent at 3 life. Instead, the attack was skipped entirely.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Sowing Mycospawn from opponent's graveyard and then skipped the attack phase.",
      "better_line": "Activate Emperor of Bones' Adapt 2 ability for {1}{B} to make it a 4/4, then attack with both Emperor of Bones (4/4) and Gran-Gran (1/2) for 5 total damage against 3 life - lethal with no blockers available.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 145,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decision_index": 186,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Kozilek's Return deals 2 damage to EACH CREATURE, not to opponents. It's a board wipe effect, not a burn spell. The player is at 3 life and the opponent is at 2 life, but Kozilek's Return cannot deal damage to players. Casting it kills the opponent's Emperor of Bones and Gran-Gran, but also means Sonnet Gramps paid 1 life from Talisman of Impulse to tap for red mana, going to 2 life themselves. Meanwhile, the opponent at 2 life has no way to be killed by this spell. The player should have looked for an actual way to deal 2 damage to the opponent, or focused on setting up a board state to attack for lethal. With 7 lands in play plus Talisman, they could cast Sowing Mycospawn (which is a creature that could attack next turn) while also developing their board. The Kozilek's Return accomplishes clearing the opponent's creatures but doesn't win the game as the player's reasoning incorrectly assumed.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Kozilek's Return, believing it deals 2 damage to each opponent, thinking it would kill the opponent at 2 life.",
      "better_line": "Cast Sowing Mycospawn to develop a creature threat that could attack for lethal next turn, while also searching for a land. Kozilek's Return could still be cast if needed to clear blockers, but the priority should be establishing a lethal attacker since Kozilek's Return doesn't hit players.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 146,
      "player": "Sonnet Gramps",
      "decision_index": 188,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The reasoning for casting Kozilek's Return is fundamentally wrong - it deals damage to creatures only, not players, so it cannot 'finish them off' at 2 life. While killing Emperor of Bones is useful, the player should have played a land first (Forest from hand) before casting spells, following proper sequencing. The misunderstanding of the card suggests the player may have cast it primarily for the wrong reason, though it does have board value.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Kozilek's Return before playing a land, believing it would deal damage to the opponent at 2 life.",
      "better_line": "Play Forest first (land before spells), then cast Kozilek's Return to clear Emperor of Bones. Understand that Kozilek's Return only hits creatures and plan accordingly for a different win condition.",
